          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"bronte-jane-eyre-b57472f62694\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":0,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.97,\"analysis\":\"Jane directly and deliberately avows that she loves her addressee specifically “as a sister.” The utterance presents an emotional and relational attitude as true within a reasoned explanation. It neither undertakes a new commitment nor has the weakly propositional, reflex-like character of an exclamation.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The avowal affirms an existing bond, but its explicit sisterly qualification and argumentative setting provide little basis for performative undertaking force.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Jane directly addresses St. John within dialogue, stating “as a sister, I love you” as part of her explanation of why she might have ventured abroad with him.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"The declaration is carefully qualified as sisterly love and embedded in Jane’s reasoned refusal to accept that she is obliged to go to India. It distinguishes genuine admiration, confidence, and familial affection from the marital or vocational commitment St. John seeks. Thus it is principally an avowal of an existing feeling, not an acceptance of his proposed relational undertaking.\" ,\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“because I admire, confide in, and, as a sister, I love you”\",\"supports\":\"The coordinated mental and emotional predicates, especially the explicit sisterly qualification, strongly support a truth-conditional avowal of affection.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“There is no dishonour, no breach of promise, no desertion in the case. I am not under the slightest obligation to go to India”\",\"supports\":\"Jane explicitly denies obligation, making it unlikely that the love utterance undertakes or renews the commitment under discussion.\",\"confidence\":0.98},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The utterance appears within an extended, syntactically controlled explanation of what Jane would have risked and why she expects not to survive the climate.\",\"supports\":\"The deliberative argumentative form weighs against exclamatory or reflex-like force.\",\"confidence\":0.97},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"supplied_metadata\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The occurrence is located in Chapter XXXV of Charlotte Brontë’s Jane Eyre.\",\"supports\":\"The location identifies the late-novel context relevant to Jane’s dispute with St. John over India and marriage.\",\"confidence\":1},{\"evidence_id\":\"e5\",\"source\":\"background_knowledge\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"In Chapter XXXV, St. John presses Jane to accompany him to India as his wife; Jane distinguishes her sisterly affection for him from the kind of love and marital union he requests.\",\"supports\":\"The wider scene confirms that this avowal explains and limits Jane’s affection rather than accepting a marital undertaking.\",\"confidence\":0.96}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":true,\"familiarity\":\"extensive\",\"confidence\":0.96,\"contribution\":\"Knowledge of St. John’s marriage-and-India proposal clarifies the contrast between Jane’s sincere sisterly affection and her refusal of the commitment he seeks.\"},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"Truth-conditional avowal naturally captures the core force. The contextual work performed by the sisterly qualification—affirming affection while refusing marriage—does not require an additional core-force category.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
